s. It distributes its products through direct-to-store delivery, independent distributors, supermarkets, convenience stores, drug stores, nutritional stores, food service providers, and mass merchants, as well as natural food stores, fitness centers, mass market retailers, vitamin specialty stores, club stores, gyms, and e-commerce platforms. The company was formerly known as Vector Ventures, Inc. and changed its name to Celsius Holdings, Inc. in January 2007. Celsius Holdings, Inc. was founded in 2004 and is headquartered in Boca Raton, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John  Fieldly CPA | CEO & Chairman | Age 45 | Compensation: $3.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $1.7M ranks in the top quartile among Consumer Defensive peers, improving at 69%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $216K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $19K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10). Recent headlines include layoff or workforce-reduction announcements.","financial_summary":["Celsius Holdings, Inc. is a mid-cap consumer defensive business with a $8.1B market cap on $2.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +57% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder. Operating margins are expanding by +2.0pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at -2% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 43%.","The business throws off $323M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 13 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.33\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 2.9\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.1%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 29% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 44% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.43909832113356256},{"text":"Beneish M-Score flags potential earnings manipulation \u2014 a question of whether the reported numbers can be trusted","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"ROIC sits in the bottom decile among Consumer Defensive peers \u2014 no moat advantage to speak of","sev":"red","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (12.1% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Consumer Defensive peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Recent layoff news \u2014 often the market\u2019s first signal that growth is under pressure or costs are being rebased","sev":"amber","cat":"news"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"GLP-1 and health-trend demand destruction \u2014 weight-loss drugs and wellness shifts are structurally reducing consumption in snack, beverage, and packaged food categories.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 insiders sell for many reasons but buy for only one","Large ope